t(1, 590, """The student of the Prophet ﷺ, the second caliph (may Allah be pleased with him), who drew everything from the Prophet ﷺ, and whose mind and thought were formed by the teaching of the Prophet ﷺ. The Companion, the second caliph of the Muslims, Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him), lived and died a student of the Prophet ﷺ. In his beautiful qualities, nothing of his awareness, his thought, his perception, his justice and his strength in the truth, nothing in all those qualities, has its origin in himself. Rather, it is a firebrand he took and lit, and a sweet handful of water he scooped himself from the river of the Prophet ﷺ. What was in him is no more than a drop he took from the presence of the Prophet ﷺ.

Without the Prophet ﷺ, none of the Companions has any existence at all. For the Prophet ﷺ is the great spring, the all-encompassing light, and all the Companions were lamps hung around this light and great radiance.""")

h(1, 1500, """Do not care about convincing people or pleasing them. From the gems of the making of the Umari Faruqi mind""")

t(1, 2085, """World trade (or economic globalisation in its old sense, embodied in intercontinental trade) began formally and in an organised way in the first century BC, specifically with the founding of the famous Silk Road under the Chinese Han dynasty around 130 BC.

A world emerging from a great war cannot conceive of going back to before 130 BC. On the psychological level, man will not bear it. For this reason, after a great world war, the nations devoted to the world and its pleasures will not bear the idea of international cooperation collapsing.

International cooperation after a coming great world war will be equal opportunity, for the first time in 100 years.

The state that will dominate the world's oil, gas, straits and mineral wealth is the coming superpower after today's world, since oil will return to being the main cornerstone for restarting the wheel of the seventies, in which oil was the master of industry alongside traditional raw materials, and a form of production based on""")

t(2, 60, """medium technology, unlike today's ultra-modern technology, which is coming very close to completion so that, once the plan is complete, oil becomes a substance worth zero. Then the Arabs, and with them the Muslims, would have a worth in the world = a real zero, not a figurative one. This is the aim of the rocket-speed effort of the West and the East in recent years to get rid of the political cost of Arab oil and of Arab and Russian gas. Russia itself faced this same existential danger alongside the Arabs!

For this reason, restarting the wheel of the seventies is the ideal solution for restoring the global balance in the world.

The world, West and East, is not ready to return to the Stone Age. When this world rejects the new rules that are to be laid down, it will be, by Allah's leave, either the seventies or the Stone Age. And imposing the technology of the seventies, which will restore the Arabs' strength in the world, is something that cannot be achieved without a new world emerging from the bowels of the present world, a world with a new equation, after the collapse of the current system, which has reached highly modern technological transitions and was moving strongly towards achieving technical leaps of even greater modernity and impact. The wheel was turning very fast; as I said, that wheel would have made the Arabs a nation enslaved anew in an unprecedented way.""")

h(2, 1000, """Global trade interconnection is inescapable, or else the world collapses and returns to the dark primitive ages""")

t(2, 1690, """The Prophet ﷺ to his cousin Zaynab: I am proposing you for Zayd (Zayd ibn Harithah)

Lady Zaynab: I have no desire for that, O Messenger of Allah

The revelation comes down with the words of Allah the Exalted:
"It is not for a believing man or a believing woman, when Allah and His Messenger have decided a matter, to have any choice in their matter. And whoever disobeys Allah and His Messenger has certainly strayed into clear error."

Lady Zaynab: We hear and obey Allah and His Messenger

Comment: What I mean by this post is not at all women marrying men, for the story of Lady Zaynab (may Allah be pleased with her) is a very special case involving revelation and the commands of Allah the Exalted, and for many wisdoms, never for a single one. Rather, the purpose of this post is to show the sensitivity of the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) and the female Companions (may Allah be pleased with them) in the way they responded to the commands of Allah the Exalted when they were informed of them, and in the speed of their obedience to the commands of Allah the Exalted.

The example of the speed of Lady Zaynab's (may Allah be pleased with her) response to Allah's command to marry Zayd ibn Harithah (may Allah be pleased with him) is a unique example about which much could be written.

Lady Zaynab's response to the command of Allah the Exalted and the command of the Messenger of Allah ﷺ expresses how far Lady Zaynab (may Allah be pleased with her) submitted to the divine command, while she was distinguished among women, and the distinguished woman disdains to give herself in marriage except to one who exceeds her in distinction, and this is a human nature for which women are not to be blamed.

By the post I meant the faith dimension connected to the state of the Ummah's components, men and women. For men too, their""")

t(3, 65, """Lord has given them commands and forbidden them prohibitions. So do they have the sensitivity to feel the greatness of the divine command, and respond without argument?

Lady Zaynab (may Allah be pleased with her) is an example for men and women of how sensitive the servant is to the commands of Allah the Exalted when he hears them. So do we listen to the commands of Allah the Exalted and carry them out with a high sense, so that the response is immediate? Or are the ears deaf, the eyes blind, the breasts disbelieving, and the souls declaring disobedience and rebellion against the Lord?!

Their sensitivity to the commands of Allah the Exalted was high, because TV series did not raise them""")

h(3, 750, """The upbringing of the Prophet ﷺ, not the upbringing of TV series""")

t(3, 1415, """Bare facts

It can be said that the Albanian people care about Islam as "identity, belonging and culture", but do not care about applying Islamic Sharia as a legal or political system, and clearly prefer the model of secular life and individual spiritual religious practices.

In Albanian society there is a fine difference between "not caring about the rites" and "denying religion". The Albanian people's view of Islam can be summed up in the following points:

Islam as part of national identity: Albanians, Muslims and Christians alike, take pride in seeing that their first and unifying religion is "Albanianism" (nationalism). For the majority, Islam is a family heritage, history, customs in marriage and condolences, naming children, and celebrating feasts (such as Eid al-Fitr and Eid al-Adha, which are official holidays that see wide popular celebrations).

Rejection of Sharia as law: There is complete popular consensus (not only governmental) that Albania be a civil democratic state applying European laws derived from the constitution, and there are no popular or party demands to apply the rulings of Islamic Sharia in the judiciary or in politics.

Spiritual, not formal, religiosity: The ordinary Albanian Muslim focuses on general moral values, faith in the heart and tolerance, while the idea of strict daily commitment to acts of worship such as prayer and the hijab is absent from him.

Therefore the people do not reject Islam, but adopt a "secular European Islam" that completely separates personal belief from the running of the state and society.

The reason for all this is 40 years of crushing Islamic identity, and generations growing up under the regime of Enver Hoxha and what came after it.

In February 1991 came the great turning point that opened the door to the irreversible return of religion and mosques to Albania.
When huge crowds of thousands gathered in the square of the capital Tirana and smashed the statue of the dictator Enver""")

t(4, 60, """Hoxha, the barrier of fear was completely broken among the Albanian people, and this was followed by rapid steps to lift the total ban on religion:

* Opening the historic mosques: In that same period, thousands of Albanian Muslims poured in to open the mosques that had earlier been turned into warehouses, museums and sports grounds. One of the most famous is the Et'hem Bey Mosque in the heart of the capital Tirana, where more than 10,000 Muslims gathered around it in an awe-inspiring scene to raise the adhan again and hold the prayer in public for the first time in decades.
* Officially abolishing the communist laws: The then tottering communist government was forced to strike out all the previous laws and constitutional articles that punished with prison and hard labour the mere possession of a Mushaf or a religious book, and a new constitution was issued guaranteeing full religious freedom.
* Launching the movement to rebuild mosques: With the new democratic government taking power in 1992, a national campaign supported by the Islamic world began to rebuild and restore what the communists had destroyed. The number of mosques rose from zero functioning mosques in 1990 to the hundreds of mosques and religious schools that fill Albania today.

The courage of those thousands who went out into the squares was the direct cause of saving Albania's Islamic identity from being erased and vanishing.""")

h(4, 1000, """Albania and Islam""")

t(4, 1740, """The current Chinese president [Xi Jinping] was one of the millions of young people whom Mao exiled to the mountains.
In 1969, when Xi Jinping was only 15 years old, he was sent to a remote and very poor village called "Liangjiahe" in Shandong (Shaanxi) province.

Xi Jinping spent ((((7)))) full years in the countryside as a day labourer, and his experience is very distinctive compared with the other young people:
* Life in the caves: [Xi Jinping] lived for years inside mountain caves (traditional dwellings carved into the rock in the region), slept on a mud-brick bed full of fleas, and worked in the harsh fields, carrying grain, hauling coal and clearing land.

Today, official Chinese propaganda uses this experience to present President Xi Jinping as a leader "forged by hardships" and close to the suffering of the people and the peasants. The village and the caves where he lived have become something like a political and tourist shrine inside China.""")

h(5, 250, """Fleas do not kill: It is told that!""")

t(5, 790, """Mao Zedong launched the "Up to the mountains and down to the hills" movement (Down to the Countryside Movement) to end the raging chaos caused by the Cultural Revolution and to restore control over the country.

In the late sixties of the twentieth century, specifically in 1968, Mao deported nearly 17 million young people of the Red Guards (armed youth) from the cities to rural and remote mountain areas for specific strategic and political reasons.

The main reasons behind this decision:

Controlling the chaos of the Red Guards: Mao at first encouraged the young people to lead the "Cultural Revolution" to purge the party of his opponents. But these young people formed armed factions and fought among themselves, plunging Chinese cities into a miniature civil war and chaos beyond control. Sending them to the countryside was a means of breaking up their groupings and disarming them.

Economic reconstruction: The unrest caused a complete paralysis of factories and production in the cities. In addition, the cities faced a stifling unemployment crisis and great pressure on food resources, so moving millions of consumers to the countryside eased this economic burden.

Ideological re-education: The Communist Party raised the slogan that urban youth needed "re-education at the hands of the poor and middle peasants" to learn the value of hard work and move away from bourgeois ideas.

Securing the borders: Large numbers of them were sent to remote border areas (such as Heilongjiang and Xinjiang) to strengthen China's human and defensive presence, especially with the escalation of military tensions with the Soviet Union at that time.

This movement deprived a whole generation of higher education, and they were later described as the "lost generation", as they lived in very harsh rural conditions and most of them were not allowed to return to the cities until after Mao's death in 1976.""")

h(5, 2150, """When Mao said: Enough! Their role is over! It is told that!""")

t(6, 385, """When the French occupation imposed the surname law (civil status law) in Algeria in 1882, the main aim was to dismantle the social and tribal structure of Algerian society and to make it easier to control.

These are the main reasons and aims behind this measure:

Destroying the tribal system: The tribe was the largest unit of solidarity in Algeria, and belonging to it strengthened collective resistance and national identity. Replacing the tribe's name with separate family surnames cut the tribal bonds and weakened social cohesion.

Facilitating the confiscation of land: It was difficult for the colonial administration to seize land because its ownership was collective and belonged to the tribe. Turning identity into a family and individual system made it easier to define holdings, strip Algerians of their land and grant it to European settlers.

Tightening security and administrative control: Under the system of three- and four-part names (e.g. Muhammad ibn Ahmad ibn Ali), the colonizer could not accurately count the population, track individuals, collect taxes or impose compulsory conscription. Giving each family a fixed surname provided an administrative system similar to the French one, making it easier to track Algerians.

Erasing Arab and Islamic identity: The colonial administration often deliberately gave Algerians humiliating surnames, or ones derived from animals and physical defects, which led to the distortion of family identity for later generations, cut them off from their history and noble lineages, and pushed millions into a state of self-contempt.""")

h(6, 1600, """When France entered Algeria: It is told that!""")

t(6, 2240, """History bears witness .. logic supports .. understanding confirms .. and the Sunnah of Allah the Exalted on earth, in the world and in the natures of His servants does not change""")

